// ============================================================================
// deepspatch.dl -- deep dispatch through Java service-provider registries.
//
// Resolves the reflective dispatch created by java.util.ServiceLoader (and
// API-compatible reimplementations): a call `ServiceLoader.load(I.class)`
// returns providers whose concrete classes are named in META-INF/services/I and
// are instantiated reflectively. A static call graph loses every provider, and
// with it everything downstream of a loaded service.
//
// This module binds the registered providers into the analysis so that calling
// provider methods dispatches to the real provider bodies. It is FULLY AUTOMATIC:
// it consumes the ServiceProvider(iface, provider) facts that fact generation emits
// from META-INF/services entries (BasicJavaSupport.writeServiceProviders) -- there
// are NO hand-authored registry or seed facts.
//
// Two complementary tiers, both gated on the service interface actually being used
// (MockSiteType), so nothing is seeded blindly:
//   1. COARSE (dispatch): register each provider mock as a stand-in for the service
//      INTERFACE type, so the program's own `iface.m()` call sites dispatch to every
//      registered provider -- independent of HOW the loader is invoked (works for
//      string-keyed finders that no call-site pattern can match). Adds no entry
//      points. This is the workhorse.
//   2. PRECISE (call site): at a REACHED ServiceLoader.load / Service.providers call
//      whose Class argument reifies the interface, bind the loader RESULT (Iterable
//      or Iterator) to the providers, so `for (s : loader) s.m()` also dispatches.
//
// Reflection-independent: the requested interface is recovered from the base
// fact ClassHeap_InstanceType (the type reified by an `I.class` literal), which
// fact generation always emits -- NOT from the reflection-gated ReifiedClass
// relation. So this fires on a plain analysis with no reflection flag. (It still
// only handles a *literal* Class argument; a Class.forName(...) result flowing to
// the load call would need reflection's string modelling.)
//
// Included at top level after the mocking layer (like spring-data-repo), so it
// uses the mainAnalysis.* component-qualified relations. It is preprocessed
// standalone (CPreprocessor.includeAtEnd), so it #includes the macro header it
// needs; ReifiedClass is component-internal, hence mainAnalysis.ReifiedClass.
// Guarded to analyses that instantiate mainAnalysis (SouffleAnalysis).
// ============================================================================
#include "../commonMacros.dl"

// Concrete, instantiable providers (drop abstract / interface registrations,
// which cannot be the run-time provider object).
.decl DispatchableProvider(?iface:symbol, ?provider:ReferenceType)
DispatchableProvider(?iface, ?provider) :-
  ServiceProvider(?iface, ?provider),
  isClassType(?provider),
  !ClassModifier("abstract", ?provider).

// --- COARSE TIER: register each provider as an implementation of the SPI -----
// The precise call-site binding below only fires at a matched ServiceLoader.load
// / Service.providers call with a Class-literal argument. Real SPI loading is
// often string-keyed and routed through custom finders (javax.xml FactoryFinder,
// per-project ObjectFactory.newInstance(String), Class.forName(name)) that no
// call-site pattern can match -- so no LOAD-SITE binding is possible there.
//
// But we do NOT need the load site: the point of an SPI is that the program then
// USES the loaded object THROUGH the service interface (`Renderer r = ...; r.m()`).
// Reaching r.m() is a DISPATCH problem, not an entry-point problem -- seeding the
// provider's constructor as a root makes the ctor reachable but never reaches m().
// So instead we make each registered provider mock a valid stand-in for the
// service INTERFACE type: MockObjectForType(providerMock, iface). Wherever the
// interface is mocked (its fields, parameters, receivers -- the central wiring in
// mocking-core section 5), the provider mock is now among the values, so the
// program's own `iface.m()` call sites dispatch to the provider's m(). This adds
// NO entry points and mocks NO uncalled methods' arguments; it reaches exactly the
// SPI methods the program invokes. It also overrides the representative-subtypes
// policy's "one impl per interface" choice for these specific registered providers
// (the whole reason the registry is needed: name ALL the providers, not just one).
// Gated on the interface actually being used (MockSiteType).
MockObjectForType(?mock, ?iface) :-
  DispatchableProvider(?iface, ?provider),
  MockSiteType(?iface),
  ProviderMockValue(?provider, ?mock).

// Service-loader factory methods: load(Class[,ClassLoader]) whose result iterates
// the providers of the requested interface. The standard JDK loader, plus
// API-compatible reimplementations that honour the META-INF/services contract.
// Extend this relation to teach deepspatch a new loader implementation.
.decl ServiceLoadMethod(?method:Method)
// (a) The standard JDK ServiceLoader entry points (public platform API -- these are
// standard signatures, NOT benchmark-specific class names).
ServiceLoadMethod("<java.util.ServiceLoader: java.util.ServiceLoader load(java.lang.Class)>").
ServiceLoadMethod("<java.util.ServiceLoader: java.util.ServiceLoader load(java.lang.Class,java.lang.ClassLoader)>").
ServiceLoadMethod("<java.util.ServiceLoader: java.util.ServiceLoader loadInstalled(java.lang.Class)>").

// (b) GENERAL detection of ServiceLoader REIMPLEMENTATIONS -- no hardcoded class
// names. Many programs (especially ones predating java.util.ServiceLoader, Java 6)
// ship their own loader that follows the same contract: read the resource path
// "META-INF/services/<interface>" and reflectively instantiate the listed classes.
// Any method that (i) uses a "META-INF/services/..." string constant in its body and
// (ii) takes a java.lang.Class as its first parameter is such a loader for that Class
// -- structurally identified, so it generalizes to any reimplementation. Methods that
// instead take a String class-name (e.g. a FactoryFinder/ObjectFactory) are NOT matched
// here; those are string-keyed and covered by the coarse tier above.
ServiceLoadMethod(?method) :-
  AssignHeapAllocation(?heap, _, ?method),
  isStringConstant(?heap),
  match("META-INF/services/.*", ?heap),
  FormalParam(0, ?method, ?param),
  Var_Type(?param, "java.lang.Class").

// The result type is an Iterable whose iteration yields providers; iterating it
// (`for (S s : loader)`) compiles to loader.iterator().next(). We model these
// returns with an ArrayList container below.
.decl ServiceLoaderIterableReturn(?t:Type)
ServiceLoaderIterableReturn("java.util.ServiceLoader").
ServiceLoaderIterableReturn("java.lang.Iterable").

// A reached service-load call: recover the requested interface from the Class
// argument's points-to set and capture the result variable and its static type.
// The interface is read off the base fact ClassHeap_InstanceType -- the type that
// the argument's `I.class` heap value reifies -- so no reflection support is
// needed (the class heap flows to the argument through ordinary points-to).
.decl ServiceLoadCall(?ctx:mainAnalysis.configuration.Context, ?ret:Var, ?iface:ReferenceType, ?retType:Type)
ServiceLoadCall(?ctx, ?ret, ?iface, ?retType) :-
  ServiceLoadMethod(?method),
  mainAnalysis.ReachableContext(?ctx, ?inMethod),
  Instruction_Method(?invo, ?inMethod),
  MethodInvocation_Method(?invo, ?method),
  ActualParam(0, ?invo, ?clsArg),
  mainAnalysis.VarPointsTo(_, ?clsVal, ?ctx, ?clsArg),
  ClassHeap_InstanceType(?clsVal, ?iface),
  AssignReturnValue(?invo, ?ret),
  Var_Type(?ret, ?retType).

// Canonical per-provider mock value, held in a helper relation so the macro calls
// below get a plain variable (nesting MOCK_OBJECT_OF_TYPE inside MOCK_OBJECT_FOR_TYPE
// does not expand cleanly under the C preprocessor).
// UNCONDITIONAL over registered providers -- this is the sound model, not waste: a
// class listed in META-INF/services/I IS instantiated by its loader at run time,
// whether or not the analysis has independently observed the interface being used.
// Materializing the mock (and, via the always-on ctor-mocking, running its ctors)
// mirrors that guaranteed instantiation. (An earlier attempt to gate this on
// MockSiteType(iface) regressed recall by ~10pp: the unconditional minting also
// bootstraps the MockSiteType closure, so gating it starved the coarse tier below.)
.decl ProviderMockValue(?provider:ReferenceType, ?mock:Value)
ProviderMockValue(?provider, cat(?provider, "::MockObject")) :-
  DispatchableProvider(_, ?provider).

// Mint the provider mock into the global mock registries (MockObject /
// MockObjectForType are the top-level registries read by the rest of the
// analysis; mainAnalysis_MockValueConsMacro registers the value's type). Uses
// the canonical per-type mock value, shared with any other layer that mocks the
// same type. Expanded manually instead of the MOCK_OBJECT_FOR_TYPE macro, which
// is defined in mocking-core.dl and not visible when this file is preprocessed
// standalone.
MockObjectForType(?mock, ?provider),
MockObject(?mock, ?provider),
mainAnalysis_MockValueConsMacro(?mock, ?provider) :-
  ProviderMockValue(?provider, ?mock).

// Bind the loader result as a container of the providers. The loader (Iterable)
// is modelled as an ArrayList whose backing array holds the provider mocks, so
// `for (S s : loader) s.m()` -- i.e. loader.iterator().next().m() -- dispatches
// to each provider's m(). Mirrors the Spring-Data repository List model.
mainAnalysis_MockValueConsMacro(?list, "java.util.ArrayList"),
mainAnalysis_MockValueConsMacro(?arr, "java.lang.Object[]"),
mainAnalysis.VarPointsTo(?h, ?list, ?ctx, ?ret),
mainAnalysis.InstanceFieldPointsTo(?h, ?arr, ?edField, ?h, ?list),
mainAnalysis.ArrayIndexPointsTo(?h, ?mock, ?h, ?arr) :-
  ServiceLoadCall(?ctx, ?ret, ?iface, ?retType),
  ServiceLoaderIterableReturn(?retType),
  DispatchableProvider(?iface, ?provider),
  ProviderMockValue(?provider, ?mock),
  Field_DeclaringType(?edField, "java.util.ArrayList"),
  Field_SimpleName(?edField, "elementData"),
  ?list = cat("<service-loader-list ", cat(?iface, ">")),
  ?arr  = cat("<service-loader-arr ", cat(?iface, ">")),
  mainAnalysis.isImmutableHContext(?h).

// --- Iterator-returning loaders (a reimplementation whose load method returns a
//     bare java.util.Iterator over the providers) ------------------------------
// These return a bare java.util.Iterator, so there is no iterator() hop to route
// through a container. We bind the returned iterator to a synthetic per-interface
// iterator value, then model next() on it to yield each provider mock directly.
.decl ServiceLoaderIteratorReturn(?t:Type)
ServiceLoaderIteratorReturn("java.util.Iterator").

// The returned var points to a synthetic service-iterator value for ?iface.
.decl ServiceIteratorValue(?itVal:Value, ?iface:ReferenceType)
ServiceIteratorValue(?itVal, ?iface) :-
  ServiceLoadCall(_, _, ?iface, ?retType),
  ServiceLoaderIteratorReturn(?retType),
  ?itVal = cat("<service-iterator ", cat(?iface, ">")).

mainAnalysis_MockValueConsMacro(?itVal, ?retType),
mainAnalysis.VarPointsTo(?h, ?itVal, ?ctx, ?ret) :-
  ServiceLoadCall(?ctx, ?ret, ?iface, ?retType),
  ServiceLoaderIteratorReturn(?retType),
  ServiceIteratorValue(?itVal, ?iface),
  mainAnalysis.isImmutableHContext(?h).

// next() called on (anything aliasing) the service iterator returns the providers.
// Iterator.next() is abstract, so ordinary dispatch produces no edge/value; we
// supply the result directly.
mainAnalysis.VarPointsTo(?h, ?mock, ?callerCtx, ?to) :-
  ServiceIteratorValue(?itVal, ?iface),
  DispatchableProvider(?iface, ?provider),
  ProviderMockValue(?provider, ?mock),
  mainAnalysis.VarPointsTo(_, ?itVal, ?callerCtx, ?base),
  VirtualMethodInvocation_Base(?invo, ?base),
  VirtualMethodInvocation_SimpleName(?invo, "next"),
  AssignReturnValue(?invo, ?to),
  mainAnalysis.isImmutableHContext(?h).
